From: theycallmeboxy <82409831+theycallmeboxy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 21:30:06 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] panvk: optionally drop the raw blocks of fragment-decoded BC
 images (opt-in)

A BC image on hardware without BC texturing kept two copies: the raw
blocks in planes[0] and the decoded texels that sampled views read. On the
fragment-decode path the raw blocks are only read again for copies out of
the image: uploads decode straight from the source buffer. Keeping them
costs 25-50% of every BC texture's memory, which decides whether a game
fits on an 8 GB device: Risk of Rain 2 had 0.88 GB of raw blocks next to
1.92 GB of decoded texels when it ran out of memory.

Images on the fragment path now take only the decoded copy's memory
(bc_decoded at offset 0; planes[0] aliases it and is never written).
Uploads skip the raw copy, copies between BC images copy decoded texels
(whole levels byte for byte, exact; partial regions by a nearest blit of
the shadow images, which re-encodes AFRC),
copies into such an image from a BC image with raw blocks decode from the
source's blocks, and zero-initialized images need no decode. Reading the
blocks back (copies to memory or to non-BC images) is not possible; it is
skipped with a one-time warning. Images the app may view or map as blocks
keep them: linear tiling, BLOCK_TEXEL_VIEW_COMPATIBLE, sparse, alias and
disjoint.

Opt-in with PANVK_BC_DROP_RAW=1, off by default: reading the blocks back is
valid Vulkan (DXVK creates every texture with TRANSFER_SRC), so dropping
them is not conformant. PanProbe's bc_decode raw-readback check fails with
it on; its copy and blit checks pass for all 16 BC formats either way.

2048x2048 with mips: BC1 8.0 -> 5.3 MiB, BC3 16.0 -> 10.7 MiB, BC7
10.7 -> 5.3 MiB (native size). With it on, Risk of Rain 2 reaches gameplay
(peak 3.4-3.95 GB instead of running out after the intro); 15 other games
boot with no skipped copies.

